              "id": "8ef3291d-f870-325c-b2c0-63496e85c704",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nA \"Vulnerable Hash Algorithm\" vulnerability refers to the use of a weak cryptographic hash function, such as MD5, in a security context. MD5 is considered to be a weak hash function as it is susceptible to hash collisions, where different inputs produce the same hash output. This can lead to various security issues, such as data integrity violations and password cracking.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a stronger hash function, such as SHA-256 or SHA-3. These hash functions are currently considered to be secure and are not susceptible to known practical collision attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of MD5 with a stronger hash function. Here is an example of how you might do this using SHA-256:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/sha.h>\n\nstd::string sha256(const std::string str)\n{\n    unsigned char hash[SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];\n    SHA256_CTX sha256;\n    SHA256_Init(&sha256);\n    SHA256_Update(&sha256, str.c_str(), str.size());\n    SHA256_Final(hash, &sha256);\n    std::stringstream ss;\n    for(int i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++)\n    {\n        ss << std::hex << std::setw(2) << std::setfill('0') << (int)hash[i];\n    }\n    return ss.str();\n}\n\n// Usage\nstd::string hsaco = \"your data here\";\nstd::string hash = sha256(hsaco);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the OpenSSL library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
