            {
              "id": "82c44af4-df2b-3eb4-aa7a-e910ad90f887",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the use of non-cryptographic or weak random number generators can lead to vulnerabilities. This is because these generators can produce predictable values, which can be exploited by an attacker. For instance, the `rand()` function in C++ is a weak random number generator as it uses a deterministic algorithm. If an attacker can predict the sequence of random numbers generated by `rand()`, they can potentially exploit this predictability to compromise the system.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a strong, cryptographic random number generator. In C++, this can be achieved by using functions provided by the `<random>` library, such as `std::random_device` or `std::mt19937`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of `rand()` with a cryptographic random number generator. Here is an example of how to do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> distr(0, 100);\n\nint random_number = distr(gen);\nstd::ignore = random_number;\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to seed the Mersenne Twister algorithm (`std::mt19937`), which is a strong random number generator. `std::uniform_int_distribution<>` is then used to generate a random number in the range [0, 100].\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Insecure Randomness](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Insecure_Randomness)\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e033b0bc-afd4-3a1d-a01b-69fc2d1c3c21",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using system time or a static value to seed randomness can lead to predictable random numbers, which is a security vulnerability. This is because if an attacker can predict the seed value, they can predict the sequence of random numbers that will be generated, which can lead to various types of attacks, such as brute force attacks.\n\nIn the given code example, `srand(0)` is used to seed the random number generator. This is a static value, which means that every time the program is run, the same sequence of random numbers will be generated. This makes the random numbers predictable, which is a security vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a truly random value to seed the random number generator. One way to do this is to use the current time, but this is still predictable to some extent. A better way is to use a value that is more difficult for an attacker to predict, such as the process ID or a value from a hardware random number generator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code fix that uses the process ID to seed the random number generator:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <ctime>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\nint main() {\n    srand(getpid() ^ time(NULL));\n    // rest of the code\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- cstdlib\n- ctime\n- unistd.h\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS149435"
                ]
              }
            }
